(A timer measures how long a job takes, and the clock reads today's date
 and time)

Print "Starting job...".
Create a timer called 'job timer'.
Start the 'job timer'.

(Waiting stands in for the job's work)
Wait 1 second.
A number called 'seconds so far' is the 'job timer's elapsed seconds.
Print "Seconds elapsed so far: {seconds so far}".

Wait 500 milliseconds.
Stop the 'job timer'.

A number called 'milliseconds taken' is the 'job timer's duration in milliseconds.
Print "Finished the job in {milliseconds taken} milliseconds.".

(A timer also keeps the unix time it started and stopped at)
A number called started is the 'job timer's 'start time'.
A number called stopped is the 'job timer's 'end time'.
Print "Started at unix time {started}, stopped at unix time {stopped}.".

Get current time into now.
Print "Today is {now's year}-{now's month:02}-{now's day:02} and the time is {now's hour:02}:{now's minute:02}:{now's second:02}.".

(The current time can be read where it is needed, without a name)
Print "It is currently hour {current time's hour} of the day.".
